WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:08.658
A payment service has three kinds of instruction: card, SEPA transfer and wallet payout.

2
00:00:08.658 --> 00:00:10.351
They almost never change.

3
00:00:10.567 --> 00:00:14.451
But new jobs for them keep coming: a fee, a bank export.

4
00:00:14.451 --> 00:00:17.364
Each job needs its own code for each kind.

5
00:00:17.567 --> 00:00:21.430
So picture a grid: one row per kind, one column per job.

6
00:00:21.430 --> 00:00:23.361
Where should the cells live?

7
00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:29.619
The usual answer is by row: each class holds its own fee and export methods.

8
00:00:29.833 --> 00:00:31.875
Then someone asks for a report.

9
00:00:31.875 --> 00:00:34.839
A new column means editing all three classes.

10
00:00:35.033 --> 00:00:37.577
A visitor stores the grid by column.

11
00:00:37.577 --> 00:00:43.089
Each job is one class, with one method per kind, behind one visitor interface.

12
00:00:43.300 --> 00:00:47.820
A new report is one new class, and the instruction classes stay put.

13
00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:50.545
Each just needs one small method: accept.

14
00:00:50.733 --> 00:00:54.082
Your code holds a plain Instruction of unknown kind.

15
00:00:54.082 --> 00:00:58.718
One Java call can only choose by the class of the object it's called on.

16
00:00:58.718 --> 00:01:00.972
So how do you reach the right cell?

17
00:01:02.733 --> 00:01:05.472
Hop one: call accept on the instruction.

18
00:01:05.472 --> 00:01:08.621
It's a wallet payout, so Wallet's accept runs.

19
00:01:08.621 --> 00:01:09.921
That picks the row.

20
00:01:10.133 --> 00:01:15.199
Hop two: it calls visitWallet on the visitor, whose class picks the column.

21
00:01:15.199 --> 00:01:16.685
Fee visitor, fee cell.

22
00:01:16.685 --> 00:01:18.644
Export visitor, the next one.

23
00:01:18.800 --> 00:01:23.159
That's double dispatch: accept picks the kind, visit picks the job.

24
00:01:23.967 --> 00:01:25.427
The catch is a new row.

25
00:01:25.427 --> 00:01:30.444
A voucher needs an accept, and every visitor ever written needs a visitVoucher.

26
00:01:30.633 --> 00:01:33.065
That trade is the expression problem.

27
00:01:33.065 --> 00:01:36.154
Methods in the classes make kinds cheap to add.

28
00:01:36.154 --> 00:01:37.928
A visitor makes jobs cheap.

29
00:01:38.100 --> 00:01:42.442
Today a sealed interface gives you the visitor's side with less code.

30
00:01:42.442 --> 00:01:46.092
It lists all three kinds, so the compiler knows every row.

31
00:01:46.300 --> 00:01:48.883
Each job is one function with a switch.

32
00:01:48.883 --> 00:01:51.797
The switch checks the object's class itself.

33
00:01:51.797 --> 00:01:54.512
One hop, no accept, no visitor interface.

34
00:01:54.700 --> 00:02:00.381
Add a voucher, and every switch without a voucher case stops compiling, each one listed.

35
00:02:01.300 --> 00:02:05.701
In Java that's a switch, in Kotlin a when, with no default branch.

36
00:02:09.033 --> 00:02:16.229
The rule: to add jobs to a closed set of types, write one function per job that switches over a sealed type.

37
00:02:16.433 --> 00:02:17.259
Quick check.

38
00:02:17.259 --> 00:02:20.425
A new job every month, a new kind once a year.

39
00:02:20.425 --> 00:02:23.109
Should rows or columns be cheap to add?

40
00:02:25.100 --> 00:02:25.714
Columns.

41
00:02:25.714 --> 00:02:28.783
So a visitor, or today, a sealed switch.
